          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            This is the generalization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+arrow+category"} twisted arrow category
            to (∞,1)-categories.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            In the case of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasicategories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            we define the twisted arrow quasi-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Tw
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tw(C)
            to be
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Tw
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mi sSet
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Tw(C)_n = sSet((\Delta^n)^{op} \star (\Delta^n), C)
          %p
            The insertions into the components of the join determine natural transformations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi src
                  %mo :
                  %mi Tw
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} src : Tw(C) \to C^{op}
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi tgt
                    %mo :
                    %mi Tw
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} tgt : Tw(C) \to C
          %p
            By
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Kerodon"} Kerodon, tag 03K9
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Tw
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Cat
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tw : (\infty,1)Cat \to (\infty,1)Cat
          %p
            Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Delta^n)^{op} \star (\Delta^n)
            is preserved by taking opposites, this construction satisfies
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Tw
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi Tw
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tw(C) = Tw(C^{op})
          %p The opposite convention often appears in the literature,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi Tw
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mi sSet
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\overline{Tw}(C)_n = sSet((\Delta^n) \star (\Delta^n)^{op}, C)
          %p
            and comes with natural transformations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi src
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo :
                  %mover
                    %mi Tw
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{src} : \overline{Tw}(C) \to C
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi tgt
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo :
                    %mover
                      %mi Tw
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{tgt} : \overline{Tw}(C) \to C^{op}
          %p
            This satisfies
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi Tw
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi Tw
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{Tw}(C) \simeq Tw(C)^{op}
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a 1-category, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Tw
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tw(C)
            agrees with the 1-categorical version. In the quasi-category model we have an even stronger statement
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is a 1-category, there is an isomorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo :
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Tw
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi Tw
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T : N(Tw(C)) \cong Tw(N(C))
              of simplicial sets, uniquely determined by the properties that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(f) = f
              and that the source and target maps are consistent with the obvious map
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mi op
                      %mo ×
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi op
                      %mo ×
                      %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(C^{op} \times C) \cong N(C)^{op} \times N(C)
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              This is
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#Kerodon"} Kerodon, tag 03JN
          %p
            For a quasi-category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Tw
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tw(C)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi Tw
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{Tw}(C)
            are quasi-categories. This follows from the following characterization:
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Tw
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mi op
                        %mo ×
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tw(C) \to C^{op} \times C
                  is the left fibration classfied by the hom-space functor
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi C
                            %mi op
                          %mo ×
                          %mi C
                          %mo →
                          %mn ∞
                          %mi Gpd
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op} \times C \to \infty Gpd
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi Tw
                          %mo ¯
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi C
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi C
                        %mo ×
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mi op
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{Tw}(C) \to C \times C^{op}
                  is the right fibration classified the hom-space functor
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi C
                            %mi op
                          %mo ×
                          %mi C
                          %mo →
                          %mn ∞
                          %mi Gpd
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op} \times C \to \infty Gpd
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              This description of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi Tw
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{Tw}(C)
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#Lurie"} Lurie 4.2.5
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#Kerodon"} Kerodon, tag 03JQ
          %p
            This means that we have the formulas
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Tw
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi el
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi hom
                      %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tw(C) \simeq el(\hom_C) \simeq ({*} \downarrow \hom)
          %p We can say more:
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to D
              is an inner fibration of simplicial sets, then the universal map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Tw
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mo ×
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi D
                          %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} op
                        %mo ×
                        %mi D
                    %mi Tw
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tw(C) \to (C^{op} \times C) \times_{D^{\op} \times D} Tw(D)
            %p is a left fibration.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              This is
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#Kerodon"} Kerodon, tag 03JT
          .num_cor
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Tw
                    %mo :
                    %mi sSet
                    %mo →
                    %mi sSet
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tw : sSet \to sSet
              is a right Quillen functor with respect to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+for+quasicategories"} model structure for quasicategories
          .proof
            %h6#proof_4 Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              be the left adjoint.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
              preserves monomorphisms, so we need to show it preserves trivial cofibrations. The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+for+quasicategories"} model structure for quasicategories
              is a Cisinski model structure, so a map is a trivial cofibration iff it has the left lifting property against fibrations between fibrant objects, which in this case are the categorical fibrations between quasicategories.
            %p
              Suppose
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              is a trivial cofibration, and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : C \to D
              be a categorical fibration between quasi-categories. Since
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+Kan+fibrations"} left Kan fibrations
              are categorical fibrations, the previous lemma implies
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Tw
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tw(f)
              is a categorical fibration between quasicategories, so
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ⊥
                      %mi Tw
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \perp Tw(f)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊥
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L(i) \perp f
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-end"} (∞,1)-end
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-colimit"} lax (∞,1)-colimit
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+arrow+category"} twisted arrow category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+arrow+modality"} twisted arrow modality
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li#Lurie
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Formal+moduli+problems"} Formal moduli problems
            %li#Kerodon
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kerodon"}> Kerodon
                ,
                %em The Twisted Arrow Construction
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                %a{:href => "https://kerodon.net/tag/03JF"} kerodon:03JF
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
            %li#MR22
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chirantan+Mukherjee"}> Chirantan Mukherjee
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nima+Rasekh"} Nima Rasekh
                %em Twisted Arrow Construction for Segal Spaces
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.01788"}> arXiv:2203.01788
                )
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twisted_diagonal_(simplicial_sets)"} Twisted diagonal (simplicial set)
